docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:136:# Libra Workflow and Versioning
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:138:## Overview
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:158:## When to Use
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:162:## Entering a Repository
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:171:## Core Principles
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:173:### Trunk-Based Development (the design default)
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:183:- **Dev branches are costs.** Every day a branch lives, it accumulates merge risk.
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:184:- **Release branches are acceptable.** When you need to stabilize a release while `main` moves forward.
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:185:- **`main` is protected.** Libra locks `main` (and the AI-capture refs `intent` / `traces`) against destructive ops — see [Branching Strategy](#branching-strategy). Lean into that: don't fight the lock with workarounds, merge through it.
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:187:### 1. Commit Early, Commit Often
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:201:### 2. Atomic Commits
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:206:# Good: each commit is self-contained
