docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:4100:COMPATIBILITY.md:207:| rebase | partial | Replayed commits keep the original author and stamp the running user as committer (a fold — `fixup`/`squash`/`amend` — keeps the target commit's author, matching Git); a missing `user.name`/`user.email` fails closed with the same identity error as `commit` rather than writing a placeholder. `--onto <newbase> [<upstream>] [<branch>]`, `--root [--onto <newbase>]` (optional positional is `<branch>`; without `--onto` the root stays parentless and unchanged picks keep their hashes), `--autosquash` / `--no-autosquash` (explicit `--autosquash` skips the already-up-to-date shortcut; last one wins; `rebase.autosquash` does not affect non-interactive rebase), explicit `--reapply-cherry-picks`, and non-interactive P1-07 controls are supported. `--autostash` / `--no-autostash` preserve tracked index/worktree changes in an fsynced held object across success, conflict, continue, skip, and abort, restoring the staged index and unstaged worktree as separate three-way layers; a restore conflict promotes the object to the normal stash list. Repeatable `--exec <cmd>` runs after each replayed commit under a required workspace-write, network-denied sandbox; non-zero/timeout stops with `LBR-CONFLICT-002`, `--continue` retries, and `--skip` keeps the applied commit while skipping its remaining exec commands. `--update-refs` / `--no-update-refs` atomically retarget other local branches in the rewritten range using captured-tip checks, including autosquash/empty/skip mappings, while excluding branches checked out in any worktree. `--fork-point` / `--no-fork-point` select the most specific upstream-reflog tip still ancestral to HEAD, with merge-base fallback. Toggle pairs are last-wins. `--rerere-autoupdate` / `--no-rerere-autoupdate` override rerere staging for the sequence, are last-wins, and persist through `--continue`; omission inherits `rerere.autoUpdate`, and both are no-ops with rerere disabled. `--keep-empty` (default no-op), `--no-keep-empty`, and `--empty=<drop|keep>` remain supported, with Libra intentionally defaulting become-empty commits to `keep`. `-i/--interactive` and `--edit-todo` are supported (`pick`/`reword`/`edit`/`squash`/`fixup`/`exec`/`break`/`drop`, plus `--autosquash`/`rebase.autosquash`, `--root`, `--exec`, and `--autostash`; `-i --update-refs` is a usage error); `--rebase-merges` and `--empty=stop|ask` are not supported. lore.md 2.6 sequencer mutex remains symmetric across operations. Runs per-worktree (v0.19.42): `rebase_state` is keyed by `worktree_id` and the aux sidecar lives in the local gitdir, so each linked worktree rebases (and continues/aborts) independently. Submodules are a permanent non-goal (ADR-MG-01 / declined `D24`): a replay whose tree inputs record different `160000` gitlink ids is refused before any write with `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001`, and a gitlink all inputs agree on is carried into the replayed tree verbatim. Each replay uses merge's shared tree engine, so rename detection, attribute/default drivers, conflict-style refinement, and file/directory handling match `merge`; a flattened replay of an existing merge commit folds every original parent into the recursive virtual base before creating its single-parent rewritten commit. This does not preserve merge topology or implement `--rebase-merges`. Remaining unsupported interactive options (`-r`/`--rebase-merges`) fail with `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001` (D16).; `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001` diagnostic for unsupported interactive options worktree materialization is mode-aware — files are created with the entry mode's permission bits (`100755` executable, `100644` plain) under the process `umask`, atomically replaced, and the index/tree entries keep the mode (plan issues/470 FM-02). |
docs/development/plan/plan-20260819.review/codex-plan-r07.log:4101:COMPATIBILITY.md:208:| merge | partial | fast-forward, single-head three-way, and multi-head octopus merge supported; before starting any merge (including an already-up-to-date target or `--dry-run`), an unresolved index without merge state is refused with `LBR-CONFLICT-002`, preserving HEAD/index/worktree and directing resolution + `add` + ordinary `commit`; existing merge state retains continue/abort guidance. This includes criss-cross histories (MG-02): when a divergence leaves SEVERAL merge bases they are folded pairwise, in ascending hex order, into one recursive virtual ancestor that the final three-way merge uses as its base — Git's recursive strategy, so a conflict Git resolves through the ancestor is no longer reported. Inside the fold `-X ours/theirs` is disabled (as in Git), a content conflict is recorded as content with the markers widened by two per recursion level, a change/delete keeps the base version, binary content (Git's NUL-in-the-first-8000 rule) keeps the base's content or the empty blob when there is none, and symlinks/mixed entry kinds keep the base entry (absent when there is none); nesting deeper than 20 levels, or more than 32 bases at one level (checked on the bare ids before any base is loaded, and on the candidates collected while folding), is refused with `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001` instead of recursing (the fold costs one merge-base walk per already-folded base per step, so width is bounded as well as depth; `-s resolve`, `-s ours`, and `--ff-only` never fold and are never refused for width — a diverged `--ff-only` merge is refused as non-fast-forward as always). Binary content is Git's `ll_xdl_merge` rule: a NUL in the first 8000 bytes OR an input larger than `MAX_XDIFF_SIZE` (1023 MiB). `merge.conflictStyle` is resolved BEFORE a multi-base merge runs (the ancestor's content depends on it), so an invalid value stops a criss-cross merge that would otherwise be clean. The syn