4100:COMPATIBILITY.md:207:| rebase | partial | Replayed commits keep the original author and stamp the running user as committer (a fold — `fixup`/`squash`/`amend` — keeps the target commit's author, matching Git); a missing `user.name`/`user.email` fails closed with the same identity error as `commit` rather than writing a placeholder. `--onto <newbase> [<upstream>] [<branch>]`, `--root [--onto <newbase>]` (optional positional is `<branch>`; without `--onto` the root stays parentless and unchanged picks keep their hashes), `--autosquash` / `--no-autosquash` (explicit `--autosquash` skips the already-up-to-date shortcut; last one wins; `rebase.autosquash` does not affect non-interactive rebase), explicit `--reapply-cherry-picks`, and non-interactive P1-07 controls are supported. `--autostash` / `--no-autostash` preserve tracked index/worktree changes in an fsynced held object across success, conflict, continue, skip, and abort, restoring the staged index and unstaged worktree as separate three-way layers; a restore conflict promotes the object to the normal stash list. Repeatable `--exec <cmd>` runs after each replayed commit under a required workspace-write, network-denied sandbox; non-zero/timeout stops with `LBR-CONFLICT-002`, `--continue` retries, and `--skip` keeps the applied commit while skipping its remaining exec commands. `--update-refs` / `--no-update-refs` atomically retarget other local branches in the rewritten range using captured-tip checks, including autosquash/empty/skip mappings, while excluding branches checked out in any worktree. `--fork-point` / `--no-fork-point` select the most specific upstream-reflog tip still ancestral to HEAD, with merge-base fallback. Toggle pairs are last-wins. `--rerere-autoupdate` / `--no-rerere-autoupdate` override rerere staging for the sequence, are last-wins, and persist through `--continue`; omission inherits `rerere.autoUpdate`, and both are no-ops with rerere disabled. `--keep-empty` (default no-op), `--no-keep-empty`, and `--empty=<drop|keep>` remain supported, with Libra intentionally defaulting become-empty commits to `keep`. `-i/--interactive` and `--edit-todo` are supported (`pick`/`reword`/`edit`/`squash`/`fixup`/`exec`/`break`/`drop`, plus `--autosquash`/`rebase.autosquash`, `--root`, `--exec`, and `--autostash`; `-i --update-refs` is a usage error); `--rebase-merges` and `--empty=stop|ask` are not supported. lore.md 2.6 sequencer mutex remains symmetric across operations. Runs per-worktree (v0.19.42): `rebase_state` is keyed by `worktree_id` and the aux sidecar lives in the local gitdir, so each linked worktree rebases (and continues/aborts) independently. Submodules are a permanent non-goal (ADR-MG-01 / declined `D24`): a replay whose tree inputs record different `160000` gitlink ids is refused before any write with `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001`, and a gitlink all inputs agree on is carried into the replayed tree verbatim. Each replay uses merge's shared tree engine, so rename detection, attribute/default drivers, conflict-style refinement, and file/directory handling match `merge`; a flattened replay of an existing merge commit folds every original parent into the recursive virtual base before creating its single-parent rewritten commit. This does not preserve merge topology or implement `--rebase-merges`. Remaining unsupported interactive options (`-r`/`--rebase-merges`) fail with `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001` (D16).; `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001` diagnostic for unsupported interactive options worktree materialization is mode-aware — files are created with the entry mode's permission bits (`100755` executable, `100644` plain) under the process `umask`, atomically replaced, and the index/tree entries keep the mode (plan issues/470 FM-02). |
4101:COMPATIBILITY.md:208:| merge | partial | fast-forward, single-head three-way, and multi-head octopus merge supported; before starting any merge (including an already-up-to-date target or `--dry-run`), an unresolved index without merge state is refused with `LBR-CONFLICT-002`, preserving HEAD/index/worktree and directing resolution + `add` + ordinary `commit`; existing merge state retains continue/abort guidance. This includes criss-cross histories (MG-02): when a divergence leaves SEVERAL merge bases they are folded pairwise, in ascending hex order, into one recursive virtual ancestor that the final three-way merge uses as its base — Git's recursive strategy, so a conflict Git resolves through the ancestor is no longer reported. Inside the fold `-X ours/theirs` is disabled (as in Git), a content conflict is recorded as content with the markers widened by two per recursion level, a change/delete keeps the base version, binary content (Git's NUL-in-the-first-8000 rule) keeps the base's content or the empty blob when there is none, and symlinks/mixed entry kinds keep the base entry (absent when there is none); nesting deeper than 20 levels, or more than 32 bases at one level (checked on the bare ids before any base is loaded, and on the candidates collected while folding), is refused with `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001` instead of recursing (the fold costs one merge-base walk per already-folded base per step, so width is bounded as well as depth; `-s resolve`, `-s ours`, and `--ff-only` never fold and are never refused for width — a diverged `--ff-only` merge is refused as non-fast-forward as always). Binary content is Git's `ll_xdl_merge` rule: a NUL in the first 8000 bytes OR an input larger than `MAX_XDIFF_SIZE` (1023 MiB). `merge.conflictStyle` is resolved BEFORE a multi-base merge runs (the ancestor's content depends on it), so an invalid value stops a criss-cross merge that would otherwise be clean. The synthetic commit's parents are ALL the real bases folded so far (Git chains one two-parent virtual commit per step — same reachability, different object ids). The virtual ancestor's tree and synthetic commit are ONE-SHOT objects (ADR-MG-04): deliberately absent from `merge-state.json` and therefore not GC roots, so `maintenance gc` may reclaim them and `merge --restart` recomputes them; the merge state records a base only when it is a real single commit. MG-12 accepts two or more targets as an octopus merge: duplicate/reachable heads are reduced while independent parents retain command-line order; non-up-to-date octopus merges never fast-forward; the entry gate requires one common history across HEAD and every target unless unrelated histories are explicitly allowed; each target is merged against the hypothetical merge of the already-incorporated heads (not their all-tip intersection), including every criss-cross LCA, and all trees stay in memory before publication; any conflict fails atomically with LBR-CONFLICT-002 and `Should not be doing an octopus`, leaving HEAD/index/worktree and merge state unchanged; clean `--no-commit` persists every target as a GC root for ordered multi-parent `--continue` or `--abort`; `--dry-run`, autostash, hooks, `-s ours`, `-X`, unrelated histories, shortlogs, and per-tip signature verification apply (trusted unsandboxed external-driver mutations remain outside Libra's rollback boundary). P1-07b adds `-s ours` (two parents, complete current tree), repeatable `-X` favor/whitespace/renormalize options (favor and renormalize toggles independently last-one-wins; whitespace modes select the strongest comparison; normalized results retain ours-side line endings), `--allow-unrelated-histories` (virtual empty base persisted safely across non-squash conflict restart/continue), and `--log[=<n>]`/`--no-log` (CLI override of `merge.log`, explicit `-m --log` supported, non-squash resolved message persisted across continue); `-m <msg>`, `--ff-only`, `--no-ff`, `--squash` (including on conflict: HEAD stays unchanged and no merge state is recorded; resolve and stage conflicts, then plain `libra commit` creates one parent; `--continue`/`--abort`/`--restart` report `no merge in progress`), `--no-commit` (retains merge state, including conflicts, and finishes with a two-parent `merge --continue`), `--no-edit` (accepted no-op; Libra never opens an editor for merge), `--stat`/`-n`/`--no-stat` (last-wins toggle; `--stat` prints a post-merge diffstat of the merge's changes, the default is no diffstat), `--no-progress` (accepted no-op; Libra's merge renders no progress meter), `--verify-signatures` (verify the merged tip's PGP signature against the local vault key — like `tag -v`, only signatures made by this repository's vault key can be validated; no external keyring) / `--no-verify-signatures` (the default; toggle pair, last wins), `--no-rerere-autoupdate` (accepted no-op — its per-invocation override is not implemented; rerere IS auto-integrated when `rerere.enabled` is set, with staging always following `rerere.autoUpdate`), and `--no-gpg-sign` (accepted no-op; Libra's merge never signs the merge commit) supported; `merge.conflictStyle` supports `merge`/`diff3`/`zdiff3` and fails closed on unknown values; the default merge style re-diffs postimages to move common edges and runs longer than three lines outside markers, zdiff3 keeps the complete base block while trimming common postimage edges, and uniformly CRLF inputs produce CRLF marker lines; MG-11 adds `-X ignore-space-change|ignore-all-space|ignore-space-at-eol|ignore-cr-at-eol`, `-X renormalize|no-renormalize`, and strict phase-local `merge.renormalize` (text/eol attributes only; no arbitrary clean/smudge filter execution; external drivers receive renormalized but not whitespace-folded inputs); merge commits carry the configured `user.name`/`user.email` on every path (three-way, `--no-ff`, `-s ours`, `--continue`) and fail closed with the same identity error as `commit` when none is configured; Libra extensions: `--dry-run` (zero-write preview; exit 0 clean, exit 1 would-conflict), `--continue -m <msg>` (Git's `--continue` takes no arguments; Libra never opens an editor for merge, so `-m` is the only way to set a conflicted non-squash merge's message — it overrides the message recorded at merge start, and without it the stored message is replayed), and `--restart` (requires non-squash conflict state; abort then re-run the recorded target, replaying recovery-critical unrelated-history permission but not presentation/policy options); external strategies, `subtree`, explicit `-s octopus`, and strategy options outside `ours/theirs/ignore-space-change/ignore-all-space/ignore-space-at-eol/ignore-cr-at-eol/renormalize/no-renormalize`, `--rerere-autoupdate`, and `-S`/`--gpg-sign` deferred; submodules are a permanent non-goal (ADR-MG-01 / declined `D24`): a three-way merge that would have to ARBITRATE a `160000` gitlink — any side recording a different commit id than the merge base, including a side that added or removed it — is refused before any write with `LBR-UNSUPPORTED-001` naming the path, while a gitlink all three sides agree on is carried into the merge result verbatim instead of being silently dropped (`merge`, `rebase` and `cherry-pick` share one guard); `--autostash`/`--no-autostash` + `merge.autostash` hold tracked dirty state outside `stash list` across non-squash conflicts/restart and separately restore staged index + unstaged worktree, promoting a conflicting re-apply into the stash list; clean squash attempts autostash restoration as the command returns; conflicted squash instead saves the autostash directly in `stash list` without applying it, preserving the unresolved index and working tree for resolution and a plain commit before `stash pop` (save failure warns and retains the held sidecar); JSON adds `autostash: applied|stashed|kept`; lore.md 2.6 symmetric cross-op sequencer mutex remains enforced.; renames are detected once per side (MG-05, the engine `diff`/`status` share): a file renamed on one side and changed on the other merges at the new path, conflicts and their stage-1 base content are presented there, `merge.renames` / `merge.renameLimit` fall back to the `diff.*` keys, are read strictly (an unparseable value fails the merge before anything is written, at exactly the points Git parses them — never on a fast-forward, an up-to-date merge, `-s ours`, or `--squash`/`--no-commit` over a fast-forwardable history) and default to Git's 7000 with any value <= 0 meaning that default, and a notice reports the degradation past the limit; the cap itself is applied PER SIDE where Git compares the whole rename matrix and counts only the sources the merge needs, so Git keeps detecting in shapes where Libra stops (intentionally-different, measured); a rename whose source the other side replaced with a different KIND of entry takes Git's `type_changed` path: the base follows the rename to the destination while the differently typed entry survives under the old name, with no rename/delete line; a destination counts as occupied only by what survives the merge, while the path-level rename conflicts follow Git's `process_renames` (MG-06): both sides renaming to the SAME path is not a conflict at all (the base follows the file and the contents merge there), rename/rename(1to2) keeps both destinations, normally with the one merged result (Git's binary fallback applies only when an unclean result matches both the hash and mode of ours' original entry: each destination then keeps its own side's original entry; content selection, including `-X ours/theirs`, preserves the independently merged mode, so other unclean binary cases can still share one result), and resolves the old name by REMOVAL (intentionally-different: Git leaves it unmerged at stage 1 and its own comment calls that legacy — Libra removes it because `add`/`rm` match through the staged entry, so a stage-1-only path with no working-tree file falls outside the ordinary documented staging flow; it remains recoverable through an index rebuild or `update-index` plumbing) (`CONFLICT (rename/rename): <old> renamed to <a> in HEAD and to <b> in <branch>.`), rename/delete keeps the new path with the renaming side's content beside the base and is a conflict even for a pure rename (`CONFLICT (rename/delete): ... but deleted in <branch>.`), while rename/add and a destination another rename took merge the rename first and then collide as a base-less add/add (plus `CONFLICT (rename involved in collision)` when the rename's own merge was unclean) — a source the other side TYPE-changed takes Git's `type_changed` path instead: the entry survives under the old name and only the destination's modify/delete is reported; rename-involved conflict markers are one character longer and labelled `<branch>:<path>`, as Git's `extra_marker_size` makes them; with `merge.conflictStyle=diff3`, the ancestor label is `base:<source-path>` (intentionally-different: Libra retains the literal `base`, while Git uses the abbreviated ancestor commit id); directory renames are inferred at the outer merge by unique-highest destination (MG-07): `merge.directoryRenames=false|true|conflict` disables, auto-moves, or suggests-and-conflicts (default), split destinations stop with affected additions at stage 0, unknown values fail closed before merge writes (intentionally stricter than Git's ignore-and-default behavior), and JSON dry-run reports `directory-rename`; directory/file (D/F) collisions follow Git's recursive strategy (MG-04): the directory keeps the path and the file is moved to `<path>~HEAD` (ours) / `<path>~<branch>` (theirs, `/`→`_`, `_N` while taken by any file or directory of any input or of the result — a path only the base had counts, as Git's `unique_path` counts it) with Git's `CONFLICT (file/directory): directory in the way of …` line, the moved path carrying the file's side on stage 2/3 plus the base's file on stage 1 (no stage 0) — an edited file is Git's modify/delete conflict relocated to that name (Git's modify/delete line printed too, the version left verbatim), a one-sided add the pure file/directory kind, an untouched file a clean deletion, a directory of only empty trees in the way only when the base had nothing at that path (Git's verbatim adoption of a new directory), `-X ours/theirs` leaving a modify/delete under a directory a conflict, a moved symlink staying a symlink — human lines only (JSON/machine stdout stays clean) and printed only once the merge is past its preflight, conflicts reported in path order, writes and removals never following a symlinked directory, a directory replaced by a file only when nothing but this merge's own files is inside it, an ignored file at either name replaced as Git replaces it while an untracked one refuses the merge, a moved symlink target kept byte-for-byte, discarding a moved file not yet possible (`libra rm` rejects unmerged paths), a non-squash merge's `--abort` removing the moved copy and pruning emptied directories; a directory that merges to nothing is not in the way; inside a criss-cross fold the file moves to `<path>~Temporary merge branch N` like Git at `call_depth > 0`; `--dry-run` JSON adds `conflict_kinds` (`content` / `modify-delete` / `file-directory` / `rename-rename` + `original_path`). A regular `commit` after resolving or `--no-commit` finishes the merge (two parents, saved message, then clear merge state / apply autostash); `--squash` leaves `SQUASH_MSG` for the following single-parent commit. Conflict markers use Git-form labels: `<<<<<<< HEAD` / `>>>>>>> <user-spelling>` (the merge target exactly as typed) and, under `diff3`, `||||||| <merge-base abbrev7>`.; reset clears an in-progress merge and keeps its autostash in the stash list; a plain commit concludes the in-progress merge; conflict markers name the merge target as typed worktree materialization is mode-aware — files are created with the entry mode's permission bits (`100755` executable, `100644` plain) under the process `umask`, atomically replaced, and the index/tree entries keep the mode (plan issues/470 FM-02). |
